WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.189
The release of the Auschwitz report

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.239
is headline news throughout the

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.240
country.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.389
These news reports

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:09.709
explaining to the American people

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.979
what Auschwitz was and what happened

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.919
there are followed up

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.109
by op-eds, by columns

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.959
about Auschwitz and what America

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.879
has to do in the wake of

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:20.929
all of this information.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.379
Fully two thirds of European Jews

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.849
were killed by the Nazis during

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:27.719
World War Two.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.239
In a systematic, relentless process

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.489
that continues to exceed our ability

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.499
to comprehend its origins and

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.669
consequences.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.379
The final solution, which was the

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.329
Nazi plan to exterminate all

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.609
European Jews, wasn't implemented

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.469
until 1942.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.089
But Hitler's government had begun

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.309
openly dehumanizing, harassing,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.199
and attacking Jews nine years

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.189
earlier.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.929
Even when the Nazi death machine

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.339
kicked into high gear.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.319
America mostly kept its doors

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.539
closed to Jews as filmmakers

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.609
Ken Burns, Lynn Novick and Sarah

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.769
Botstein recount in the U.S.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.749
in the Holocaust, a three part

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.629
documentary series on PBS.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.799
The anxieties about urbanization,

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.529
about unlettered, untutored,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.779
relatively uneducated peoples

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.209
coming in, in large numbers, and

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.069
the sense that disease was

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.609
a problem.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.679
All of these worries were

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.539
amalgamated into

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.159
a belief that immigrants caused

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.499
these problems and thus immigration

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.500
should be held down.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.969
Some lobbied to open the country to

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.839
refugees in the run up to war.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.019
But anti-immigration legislation,

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.879
the economic devastation of the

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.739
depression, incredulity

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.419
toward a press that had trafficked

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.249
in false atrocity accounts during

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.229
World War One, and deep seated

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.629
anti-Semitism, especially

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:48.979
in Franklin Roosevelt's State

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.199
Department, combined to thwart

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.159
those efforts.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.439
Reason talked with Burns and Novick

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.149
about why a nation of immigrants

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.129
remains so deeply ambivalent

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.989
about newcomers and the lessons

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.059
that 21st century America

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.589
should draw from our country's

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.389
response in the lead up to the

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:06.390
Holocaust.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.539
Lynn Novick.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.769
Ken Burns, thanks for talking to

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:15.489
Reason.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.569
Thank you for having us.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.699
So the series

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.829
begins in in

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.209
the early thirties, and it's

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.979
actually you're talking about Otto

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.809
Frank, who is known to

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.399
everybody as the father of Anne

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.929
Frank and kind of the person who

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.059
brought the diary to notice.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.639
But it's in Frankfurt

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.279
and Otto Frank is

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.899
what you're talking about is his

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.879
plans, his thwarted plans

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.519
to bring his family to America.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.379
Why is it so important that, you

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.239
know, you start the series with

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.249
this question of Otto

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.229
Frank not being able to bring

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.449
his family to the United States

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.039
at the very beginning of the Nazi

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:54.549
era?

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.019
You know, we started out to make a

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.879
series about the United States

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.349
and the Holocaust and what was our

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.639
relationship to what was happening

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.929
in Europe and what did we as

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.279
Americans know and what should we do

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.109
about it just collectively?

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.969
And while we were working on the

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.859
film, which has been many years in

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.809
the making, some documents came

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.789
to light that showed

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.319
Otto Frank, Anne Frank's father,

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.489
writing to friends in the United

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.649
States asking for help to get here.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.509
And that was just extraordinary

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.949
for us to recognize that the story

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.839
of Anne Frank, we thought, I speak

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.069
for myself and I think most of us

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.179
felt this was a story about the

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.979
Holocaust that happened over there,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.599
had nothing to do with America.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.999
And here's one of the most

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.949
recognizable stories of

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.689
the Holocaust.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.339
Has a lot to do with America.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.199
So we decided to frame

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.209
our film around that idea

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.009
by telling the story of Otto Frank

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.539
and his efforts to get to America to

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.189
help our audience sort of see

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.659
themselves in this story.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.699
And in 1933, it's

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.079
the Depression.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.029
It's about a decade after

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.189
the Johnson-Reed Act, which

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.349
was the culmination of 40

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.479
years of legislative activism

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.639
or activism to shut down

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.379
immigration, particularly from

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.330
central and southern Europe.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.154
Why? Why is immigration, you

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.149
know, what what's going

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:11.589
on with the way immigration was

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.419
restricted a decade before when,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.349
you know, during the Roaring

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:15.189
Twenties?

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.169
How does that factor into what's

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.189
going on next?

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.599
So there are two important things

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.189
about immigration. One is symbolic,

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.899
like what kind of country are we?

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.879
You know, are we the Emma Lazarus?

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.709
Give us your tired, your poor nation

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.489
of immigrants?

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.379
Or are we an almost equal

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.279
reaction to that?

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:31.699
No, let us stop this.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.236
So between 1870 and 1920,

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.729
millions and millions of people come

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.099
from areas that are threatening

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.869
to the Protestant majority, and they

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.669
want to figure out a way to go back

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.649
to a an

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.989
earlier time, always impossible.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.119
And when it was a dominant,

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.979
there was a fear was that these

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.689
newcomers who from Central and

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.399
southern Europe, Catholic and

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.769
Eastern Europe, Catholic and Jews,

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.319
would would they be replacing

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.099
us?

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.509
And so the Johnson-Reed Act is an

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.879
attempt to essentially set quotas

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.949
from the countries, that's minuscule

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:07.119
quotas, from the countries that are

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.129
less desirable and bigger quotas

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.019
of people from the Northern

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.309
European.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.319
Who iron- not, ironically,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.269
but are not emigrating in the

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.009
numbers where, you know.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.499
So Germany gets big quotas,

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.179
but not that many people are coming.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.039
And then places like Italy get

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.059
very low quotas.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.379
And a lot of people want to move

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:27.380
here.

